        "original_id": "419a47c1-bc5c-5df7-bb0a-4bf30b3f2fdb",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Hill's Tavern, Central Hotel, Century Inn, National Road\n- Topics: naming history, continuous operation, transportation history\n\nFacts:\n- The National Road was built between 1811 and 1837.\n- The National Road stretched 620 miles and connected the Potomac River and the Ohio River.\n- The National Road served as a main transport route to the West for thousands of settlers.\n- In the early 1900s, Hill's Tavern was known for a period as the Central Hotel.\n- Hill's Tavern, later called the Century Inn, had been the oldest tavern in continuous use on the National Road.\n- A fire ended the Century Inn's 221 years of continuous operation.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "7c1c3b7e-212e-5910-9824-b43b6818a444",
        "text": "Page: Hill's Tavern. Sentence: For a period in the early 1900s, the inn was known as Central Hotel.\nPage: Hill's Tavern. Sentence: Now called the Century Inn, it had been the oldest tavern in continuous use on the National Road, until the fire brought an end to its 221 years of continuous operation.\nPage: National Road. Sentence: Built between 1811 and 1837, the 620 mi road connected the Potomac and Ohio Rivers and was a main transport path to the West for thousands of settlers.",
